✅ 1. @Value
✅ 2. @ConfigurationProperties
✅ 1. @Value Annotation (for individual values)
Used to inject single values from properties/yml file into fields.

📁 application.properties
properties
Copy
Edit
myapp.name=ChatGPT
myapp.version=1.0
📦 Java Class using @Value
java
Copy
Edit
import org.springframework.beans.factory.annotation.Value;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;

@Component
public class AppInfo {

    @Value("${myapp.name}")
    private String name;

    @Value("${myapp.version}")
    private String version;

    public void printInfo() {
        System.out.println("App: " + name + " | Version: " + version);
    }
}
✅ Pros:
Simple for injecting a few properties

Good for constants

❌ Cons:
Not suitable for grouped or nested config

No auto-binding of complex types like lists/maps

✅ 2. @ConfigurationProperties (for groups of values)
Used to bind a group of related configuration values to a Java class.

📁 application.yml
yaml
Copy
Edit
myapp:
  name: ChatGPT
  version: 1.0
  features:
    enabled: true
    max-users: 100
📦 Java Class using @ConfigurationProperties
java
Copy
Edit
import org.springframework.boot.context.properties.ConfigurationProperties;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;

@Component
@ConfigurationProperties(prefix = "myapp")
public class MyAppConfig {

    private String name;
    private String version;
    private Features features;

    public static class Features {
        private boolean enabled;
        private int maxUsers;

        // getters and setters
        public boolean isEnabled() { return enabled; }
        public void setEnabled(boolean enabled) { this.enabled = enabled; }

        public int getMaxUsers() { return maxUsers; }
        public void setMaxUsers(int maxUsers) { this.maxUsers = maxUsers; }
    }

    // getters and setters
    public String getName() { return name; }
    public void setName(String name) { this.name = name; }

    public String getVersion() { return version; }
    public void setVersion(String version) { this.version = version; }

    public Features getFeatures() { return features; }
    public void setFeatures(Features features) { this.features = features; }
}
✅ Usage in any service:
java
Copy
Edit
@Autowired
private MyAppConfig config;

public void printConfig() {
    System.out.println(config.getName());
    System.out.println(config.getFeatures().getMaxUsers());
}
✅ Pros:
Great for grouped/nested config

Auto-binds lists, maps, complex objects

Clean separation of config

❌ Cons:
Requires getters/setters

Requires @EnableConfigurationProperties in some cases (not needed with @Component in Spring Boot)

🎯 When to Use What?
| Situation                            | Use                        |
| ------------------------------------ | -------------------------- |
| Just 1–2 simple values               | `@Value`                   |
| Many related settings or nested data | `@ConfigurationProperties` |


